<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="9d83582c">&lt;table title=&quot;&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;8&quot; width=&quot;85%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot;&gt;
    &lt;tbody&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/delivery_process32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Delivery Processes&lt;/strong&gt; are used to define the typical approach to a stereotypical project and
                is represented via an associated breakdown structure. It provides a complete end-to-end lifecycle (for it&apos;s
                scope) and can be used as a reference for running projects with similar characteristics. Delivery processes
                are the primary mechanism for representing sequencing within the method..
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/phase32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Phases&lt;/strong&gt; represent a special type of activity that may be positioned as a separate project
                and typically ends in a major checkpoint, milestone or set of deliverables. Phases typically have well
                defined objectives and provide the basis for how the project work will be structured.
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/iteration32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Iterations&lt;/strong&gt; represent a repeatable activity often used in agile projects to cycle through
                the same set of tasks in order to refine the overall set of outputs. It is included in the method as
                convenience as any type of activity may be set to repeating.
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/activity32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Activities&lt;/strong&gt; are a grouping of related tasks or other activities into logical units of work
                to assist in the organization of the overall breakdown structure.
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/task32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Tasks&lt;/strong&gt; define assignable units of work that provide the instructions necessary to transform
                a set of inputs into outputs, typically through a series of steps. Note that tasks are defined
                independently of how they may be used in a work breakdown structure or project schedule.
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/milestone32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Milestones&lt;/strong&gt; represent significant events in a project, such as a key decision, completion
                of a significant piece of work or meeting of a major dependency.
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.default.nav_view.base/guidances/concepts/resources/capability_pattern32.png&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Capabilities&lt;/strong&gt; define the required set of content to deliver a particular service or solve a
                client issue. Capabilities are the primary mechanism for promoting consistency across service delivery
                methods while facilitating their rapid assembly and customization. To provide the required flexibility each
                capability is designed as an independently adoptable set of content that may be swapped out for equivalent
                capabilities focused on achieving the same goals with alternative techniques. Since capabilities are
                designed to be self-contained sets of content they contain all of the tasks, work products, roles and
                associated guidance needed to support that area of concern and as such also provide a convenient means of
                referencing and understanding the content for those areas.
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</attribute>
